  "notes": "A UK-garage-and-deep-house duo with pop structure — \"electronic house music with a pop structure\" in their own words. Claimed by this section rather than ceded to a house researcher, per the volume's assignment notes. Their entry documents two rhythmic treatments — \"swung 16th hats with the kick displaced off the grid in the garage mode; four-square kick with clap on 2&4 in the house mode\" — and this model states the composite: a 0.57 swing (the heaviest in the producer block), a kick anchored on 1 and 3 with a 0.30 syncopation and a third of its weight on the offbeat eighths, so a high density reads as four-square and a low one as the displaced 2-step. That composite is marked INFERRED because the research describes two modes rather than one pattern. Their learning method — \"de-constructing and copying the music\" they encountered — is why the entry insists the rhythmic detail is idiomatically precise, and it is the reason the ghost density and hat fill sit high. The harmony is the other identity: \"jazz-adjacent extended voicings\", with a cited influence set running from Bach and Debussy to Chicago house, Detroit techno, 2-step and J Dilla, which is the lowest triad weight of any producer model in this section. The hook is \"chopped, pitched vocal samples used as the melodic hook\", and the bass is \"a deep, syncopated sub-bass line with real melodic motion between chord tones — in the garage mode it plays the offbeats the kick vacates\". ⚠ The velocities, densities and registers are INFERRED.",
